Signal handling design: extend *ABORT-HOOK* to all Term/Core signals, and add
BOCL:*STOP-HOOK* / BOCL:*CONT-HOOK* for SIGTSTP/SIGCONT.  Deferred — study a few
CL implementations first, signal handling is subtle.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. BACKGROUND — POSIX default signal actions (man 7 signal)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Term   terminate the process
  Ign    ignore the signal
  Core   terminate the process and dump core
  Stop   stop (suspend) the process
  Cont   continue the process if stopped

Principle we want:
  - Term or Core (a "crash"/termination): call BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK* (signum).
  - Stop:  call a new BOCL:*STOP-HOOK* () before the process suspends.
  - Cont:  call a new BOCL:*CONT-HOOK* () when the process resumes.
  - Ign:   no hook.

Rationale for *STOP-HOOK* / *CONT-HOOK*: let the process release/restore
resource state across suspend/resume — e.g. reset the terminal out of an
ncurses/raw mode on stop and re-enter it on cont; or close a network connection
on stop (can't service it while suspended) and re-open it on cont.

  (push (function restore-terminal)    bocl:*stop-hook*)
  (push (function set-ncurse-terminal) bocl:*cont-hook*)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. CURRENT STATE (Slices 58-59)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK* exists; the crash handler (kernel.c bocl_crash_handler) runs
  it then re-raises with SIG_DFL.  GC is disabled in the handler; there is a
  re-entrancy guard; it only enters Lisp when a hook is registered.
- It is installed for a SUBSET only: SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGABRT (Core) and
  SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP (Term).  The native backtrace dump is gated on
  $BOCL_BACKTRACE.
- No *STOP-HOOK* / *CONT-HOOK* yet.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. SIGNAL TAXONOMY (catchable, by default action) — Linux/macOS differ slightly
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uncatchable (cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored): SIGKILL, SIGSTOP.
  => SIGKILL: no abort hook possible.  SIGSTOP: no stop hook possible; only the
     interactive/job-control SIGTSTP (Ctrl-Z) is catchable, and SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU.

Core  (=> *ABORT-HOOK*): SIGQUIT, SIGILL, SIGABRT, SIGFPE, SIGSEGV, SIGBUS,
      SIGTRAP, SIGSYS, SIGXCPU, SIGXFSZ, SIGEMT(BSD/macOS).
Term  (=> *ABORT-HOOK*): SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGPIPE, SIGALRM, SIGTERM, SIGUSR1,
      SIGUSR2, SIGPROF, SIGVTALRM, SIGSTKFLT(Linux), SIGIO/SIGPOLL, SIGPWR(Linux).
Stop  (=> *STOP-HOOK* via the catchable proxy SIGTSTP): SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN, SIGTTOU.
Cont  (=> *CONT-HOOK*): SIGCONT.
Ign   (no hook): SIGCHLD, SIGURG, SIGWINCH (+ SIGCONT's default is Cont not Ign).

CAUTION: "catch every Term/Core signal" is NOT obviously right —
  - SIGPIPE is very commonly left at SIG_IGN by libraries; catching it to run a
    hook + terminate would change long-standing behaviour.
  - SIGALRM/SIGVTALRM/SIGPROF/SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2/SIGIO are frequently used by
    libraries/profilers for non-fatal purposes; grabbing them by default would
    break them.
  - SIGCHLD/SIGURG/SIGWINCH are Ign by default; SIGWINCH is wanted by TUIs.
So the real design question is *which* signals bocl should claim by default,
which to leave alone, and how to let user code opt in/out per signal.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. COMPLEXITY / OPEN QUESTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Async-signal-safety: running arbitrary Lisp from a C signal handler is unsafe
  (the handler may interrupt malloc/GC/printf).  Current *ABORT-HOOK* accepts
  this as best-effort.  The robust approach most CL impls use is DEFERRED
  signals: the C handler only sets a flag (and saves siginfo); the flag is
  checked + the Lisp handler run at a safe point (between forms, on allocation,
  on the next REPL turn).  That can't help true crashes (SEGV in bad state) but
  is right for TERM/INT/TSTP/CONT/USR.  Decide: best-effort-in-handler vs
  deferred vs hybrid (deferred for Term/Stop/Cont/User, best-effort for Core).
- SIGTSTP/SIGCONT dance: SIGTSTP handler must run *STOP-HOOK*, then actually
  suspend (reset SIGTSTP to SIG_DFL and re-raise, or raise SIGSTOP), re-arming
  the handler; on resume the SIGCONT handler runs *CONT-HOOK*.  Must cooperate
  with job control (don't deadlock the shell) and with SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU.
- SA_RESTART vs EINTR: do we restart interrupted syscalls (read in the REPL) or
  surface EINTR?  Affects whether Ctrl-Z/Ctrl-C interrupt a blocking READ.
- sigaltstack: for SIGSEGV/SIGBUS (stack-overflow), the handler needs an
  alternate signal stack or it re-faults.  (Relevant to the current best-effort
  abort hook too.)
- SIGINT semantics: most CL REPLs treat Ctrl-C as an INTERACTIVE INTERRUPT into
  the debugger, NOT as terminate.  bocl currently runs *ABORT-HOOK* + terminates
  on SIGINT.  Decide whether SIGINT should instead interrupt to the REPL/debugger
  (then it is not an "abort" at all and shouldn't run *ABORT-HOOK*).
- Re-raising to preserve the default action (Term vs Core/core-dump) after the
  hook: keep the current "signal(sig,SIG_DFL); raise(sig);" so a Core signal
  still dumps core.
- Threads: hooks "may be called from a thread other than the main thread"
  (per the extension-hooks spec); signal delivery + thread choice matters once
  bocl has threads.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. STUDY PLAN — what do other implementations do?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Read the signal handling of: SBCL, CCL (Clozure), ECL, CLISP, ABCL, Allegro.
Questions per impl:
  - which signals do they install handlers for, and at what default?
  - deferred-signal mechanism? (SBCL pseudo-atomic / without-interrupts;
    sb-sys:enable-interrupt, sb-unix, sb-sys:*interactive-interrupt*).
  - SIGINT -> debugger (INTERACTIVE-INTERRUPT) vs terminate.
  - SIGTSTP/SIGCONT handling for the REPL (terminal save/restore).
  - exit vs abort hooks: SBCL sb-ext:*exit-hooks* (normal exit only) +
    *save-hooks*; CCL; ECL ext:quit hooks.  Note ours already splits
    *QUIT-HOOK* (normal) from *ABORT-HOOK* (signal).
  - sigaltstack usage; SA_RESTART policy; SIGPIPE policy (most SIG_IGN it).
Seed (from memory, verify): SBCL uses deferred signals + pseudo-atomic regions,
SIGINT -> INTERACTIVE-INTERRUPT into the debugger, sb-ext:*exit-hooks* run on
normal exit only, SIGPIPE handled/ignored.  CCL has ccl:*break-hook* and installs
its own handlers.  Most impls do NOT run arbitrary user Lisp directly from the C
handler for non-fatal signals — they defer.

